  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is well formed and justified.\n\n- **Form:** The citation sits on an explicit function with a body. Its commentary is in a fenced `:::{cite}` block, which the built-in requires.\n- **Substance:** The reason is not just difficulty. The function is an intentional guard that panics if storage code discovers graph edges, so it is expected to run zero times. Running it would break the test's invariant.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - `Domain::outgoing_edges` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/domain.rs` is a required method with no default body, as the commentary says.\n  - The sibling `fn[load_evidence]` and `fn[store_evidence]` in `impl[StoredGraph/Domain]` delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `self.node_path(node)`, which matches the claim that the delegation test uses only `node_path`.\n  - Other fixtures cover edge discovery, for example `ValidationContext::outgoing_edges` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs` and the `Memory` domain in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs`. This matches the claim that traversal fixtures exercise it separately.\n- **Redundancy:** The function has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.",
